    "result_text": "The build passes with all tests green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented rejection with 400 when an owner's email domain is on the disposable-domain blocklist, following the existing owner-validation exception pattern in the codebase.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **New exception** \u2014 `rest/advice/DisposableEmailException.java`: a `RuntimeException` carrying the rejected raw email value, mirroring the existing `InvalidEmailException`.\n\n2. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice.java`** \u2014 added `@ExceptionHandler(DisposableEmailException.class)` returning `400 Bad Request` with an `errors: [\"email\"]` property, consistent with the other email/field validation handlers.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 added a `DISPOSABLE_EMAIL_DOMAINS` set (`mailinator.com`, `tempmail.com`, `guerrillamail.com`) and a check inside the existing `normalizeEmail(...)` method. After the address is validated and lower-cased, its domain (everything after the last `@`) is compared case-insensitively against the blocklist, throwing `DisposableEmailException` on a match. Placing it in `normalizeEmail` means the rule applies to both `addOwner` and `updateOwner`, and blank/absent emails remain accepted.\n\nThe `AcceptanceTest.errorRejectsDisposableDomain` test (which posts `x@mailinator.com` and expects 400) passes, and the full suite reports `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
